WEBVTT

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:20.490
Leave it to Beaver, starring Barbara Billingsley, Hugh Beaumont, Tony Dow, and Jerry Mathers as the beaver.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.550
Boy, it's going to be real neat, huh, Beaver? Fishing and camping and messing around up in the woods?

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:29.690
Yeah!

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.530
We might even find an owl asleep in a tree and bring it home.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.740
Why don't you want to bring home an owl?

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:37.130
I don't know.

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:41.110
I'd be the only kid in the block with a real live owl.

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:43.910
Well, if we're lucky, we might find an owl.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.140
Sure, the weekend would get here.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:55.170
Have a good game?

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:57.570
How could you help having a good game on a day like this?

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:04.570
Oh, the air was so clear, you could see for miles.

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:06.570
Good, then you didn't lose any golf balls.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.020
Only three.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:09.830
Well, where are the boys?

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:11.130
Must be outside somewhere.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.370
Kids couldn't resist taking advantage of a day like this.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.450
They're taking advantage of a double feature

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:16.550
at a cartoon parade.

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:18.530
Movies on a day like this?

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:19.890
I drove them down at noon, and I said

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:20.910
you'd pick them up at 6.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:24.590
You know, somewhere back in history,

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.390
it's rumored that the Cleavers were related

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:27.530
to Johnny Appleseed.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.980
These two characters of ours can't even walk back.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:30.880
and forth to the movies.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:33.130
Eddie and Chester and Tui are with them.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:34.510
You're going to have to drop them off, too.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:35.050
Oh, great.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:52.480
Hi, Dad.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:54.410
We came out to see if you were here.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.080
Well, I've been here for 10 minutes.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.360
And you were supposed to be out here at 6 o'clock.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:01:59.860
Gee, Dad, we would have been here.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.500
but the guys didn't want to come all the way out

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.600
unless they knew you were here.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.500
Well, you know, sometimes we miss a lot of good stuff

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:06.920
just hanging around waiting for you.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:07.960
That's too bad.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.780
Well, come on, come on, go on and get the guys out here

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:11.760
now in a hurry, yeah?

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:12.480
Sure they are.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:36.650
Oh, hi, B. Well, where are the other boys?

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:37.870
I don't know.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:39.660
We'll have to get a chocolate, Brian.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:40.800
I thought I'd see if he was here.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:42.580
I just sent Wally in to look for you.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:43.300
Didn't you see him?

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:44.680
Must have gone down the other aisle.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.710
All right, now you stay right here.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:49.130
I'll get my jacket.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:51.520
All right, all right, get your jacket.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:53.960
But find Wally and tell him to get on out of here in a hurry.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:00.700
Yeah, hello, Ward.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:01.740
Hello, Fred.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:03.100
What you doing, holding up the building?

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:07.940
No, I'm just picking up the boys.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.560
Yeah, I came to pick up my big fella, Clarence.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:11.820
Oh, uh, how is Lumpy?

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:13.100
Lumpy?

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:15.360
Oh, Clarence is just fine.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.920
Weighs 198 pounds now, all of it's solid muscle.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:20.740
Hi, Daddy.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.620
Well, Clarence, say hello to Mr. Cleaver.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.060
Hello, Mr. Cleaver.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:25.640
Hello, Clarence.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.440
Gee, Daddy, you said you'd be here at 6.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:29.960
I came out three years.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.940
Time's looking for you, and you weren't here.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:33.540
Mom said you would be.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:35.800
Gosh, I missed a lot of the cartoon.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.260
Well, now, Clarence, don't upset yourself.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:40.340
Daddy will buy you a soda on the way home.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:41.620
So long, Ward.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:43.400
See you around the salt mines.

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:45.730
Now, tell me about the cartoon.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:54.290
Hi, Mr. Cleaver.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:54.970
Oh, hi, hi.

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:55.890
Hello, Mr. Cleaver.

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:57.610
Sorry to hold you up, but we had to wait

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:58.810
while the kid was hitting Toohey.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:03:59.570
That's all right.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:01.800
He wasn't hitting me, Mr. Cleaver.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:03.380
He was just shoving me around.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:05.420
Well, anyway, come on.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:06.360
Let's get going, boys.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:06.700
I can't.

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:07.910
Wait a minute.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:08.880
Where's the beaver?

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:09.920
The beaver?

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:11.280
Yeah, the beaver.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:12.860
I just sent him in to look for you.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:13.640
Didn't you see him?

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:14.720
Yeah, we saw him.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.370
Well, didn't he say anything?

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:18.060
Uh-uh.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:20.570
He just sat down and started watching the cartoon again.

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:23.890
I'd go back in and get him, Mr. Cleaver,

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:25.980
but that kid would start hitting me again.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:28.180
Oh, no, no.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:29.840
I think I'd better get him.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.950
Now, don't you fellows go away.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:34.850
I'll see that they don't, Mr. Cleaver.

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:35.690
Thank you, Eddie.

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:37.810
Now, you fellows behave yourselves.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:41.660
Don't you pull it all the way down.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:42.460
It helps you looks.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:43.300
Oh, oh, oh.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.860
Oh, we already got hit there.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:49.880
Hi, Eddie.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.120
I can do it by myself.

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:55.870
Hi, B.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:04:59.230
Hi.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.500
We went in the theater looking for you.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.260
Cartoon was all so I come out.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:07.180
My mother's going to be awfully sore if I get home late.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:09.620
Gee, we can't go without my dad.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:11.760
What are we going to do?

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:13.700
Well, let's go back in and look for him.

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:16.100
When we get inside, let's spread out.

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:18.520
We're just going to go in.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:19.380
We've been in here before.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:47.560
Supper's almost ready.

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:49.750
Oh, we had 12 fun and dad in the movies.

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:56.900
Well, uh, I'll get supper on the table.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:05:59.960
You know, you...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.740
You wouldn't have had any trouble finding me if you'd

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:02.960
been where you were supposed to be.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:04.930
Well, I'll tell you one thing.

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:07.970
This is the last Saturday we go through this movie routine.

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:11.020
Gee, Dad, what's wrong with going to the movies?

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.020
Wally, do you realize you kids went in that theater

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:14.780
today at noon?

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:17.430
It's now 20 minutes after 6.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:19.910
You spent over six hours today sitting

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:21.290
in that stuffy movie theater.

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:24.370
Yeah, they sure give you a lot for your 35 cents, don't they?

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:27.770
That's not the point I'm making, Beaver.

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:29.910
Look, I should think you

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.880
boys would want to be outside today, out in the fresh air

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:34.860
where you can breathe.

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:37.400
Gee, Dad, we can breathe all week.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:39.180
We only get to go to the movies on Saturday.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:47.920
Look, Wally, you don't have to go to the movies

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:49.080
every Saturday, do you?

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:50.580
Now, look, to take today, for instance,

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:53.000
why couldn't you kids have gone over to the athletic field

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:53.800
and played baseball?

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:55.480
Gee, Dad, they put up a fence.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.200
They got a guy to chase his kids away.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:59.920
Oh, come now, Beaver, why would they have a man

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.040
at the athletic field to chase kids away.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:03.480
Well, he didn't explain it.

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:05.280
He just tells us to get out of there.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:07.800
Well, all right, then.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:09.340
All right, you could have gone over to the park

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:11.100
and played mumbledy-peg.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:14.680
Gee, Dad, they arrest you if they catch you with a knife.

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:17.540
All right.

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:20.200
All right, you boys better go on upstairs

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:21.440
and get washed up for supper.

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:29.940
Is that kid hitting Tui in the movie?

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.060
I don't know.

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:32.510
I guess she thought he knew him.

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:40.850
What's up now?

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:46.470
June, can you tell me why they'd have a man at the athletic field to chase kids away?

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:47.990
Didn't you read about that?

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:51.060
They want the grass to look nice for the dog show next month.

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:52.600
Oh.

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:56.290
Boy, don't be upset about the movies.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:58.380
I'll pick the boys up next week.

00:07:59.180 --> 00:07:59.960
They're not going to the...

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:04.280
Next week, Wally and the Beaver are going to enjoy a well-planned weekend

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:06.940
out of doors where they can get some fresh air.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:10.080
They won't get on the athletic field unless we enter them in the dog show.

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:13.640
June, I'm serious about this.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:15.720
They need to get out of doors.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:18.620
They ought to be doing the kind of things I used to do when I was a kid.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.340
You know what I'm gonna do?

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:24.280
Next weekend, I'm gonna take them up to Friends Lake

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:25.880
for an overnight camping and fishing trip.

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:27.300
Oh, boy, that's wonderful.

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:29.980
Well, you haven't had time to do that in a long time.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:30.900
No, I haven't.

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:32.660
They're upstairs washing up.

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:33.840
I'm going to go up and tell them right now.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:35.360
Don't be too long.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:36.160
Supper's ready.

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:39.600
Oh, say, dear, uh, you're not going to mind

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:41.020
being alone next weekend, are you?

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:42.500
Of course not, dear.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:44.400
While you're in the great outdoors,

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:45.820
I'll go to a movie or something.

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:57.990
Yeah, yeah, Patty, Saturday, our dad's

00:08:57.990 --> 00:08:59.370
going to take us up to Friends Lake.

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:04.060
Wally, I'm going to make fire by knocking two stones together.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:05.640
Cut it out, will you, Peef?

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:09.540
Sad Eddie, sure he promised.

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:12.270
Huh, my Pop's the same way.

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:14.710
He promises you something to keep you quiet.

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:16.930
Saturday comes along, you remind him of it,

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:19.390
he acts like you've got rocks in your head.

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:21.590
Dark ass not like that.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:23.900
When he promises you something, he means it.

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:26.680
Next week at the movies.

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:29.240
Like fun you will, Eddie.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:30.720
Eddie?

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:39.380
We're going to take his camping, isn't he?

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:40.930
Sure he is, Beef.

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:42.360
You know how Eddie is.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:43.940
Whenever you tell him something good,

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:46.300
he's got to tell you something bad to spoil it for you.

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:50.620
Well, we got an early start in the morning.

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:53.650
I want to get up to the lake by 9 at the latest.

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:56.170
With no problem persuading the boys

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:57.150
to go to bed early tonight.

00:09:57.910 --> 00:09:58.790
Beaver was cute.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:09:59.980
He didn't want to get undressed.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.440
he said he wanted to practice sleeping with his clothes on.

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:06.320
Hey, Jim, why don't you come along with us?

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:10.440
Well, I knew you'd ask me when it was too late for me to accept,

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:12.180
but I didn't think it'd be this too late.

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:14.500
Well, dear, you know you're perfectly welcome.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:17.540
Well, thank you, dear, but I'm too contented here.

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:21.600
I don't think I could bring myself to leave my garbage disposal for that long.

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:25.540
Oh, uh, here.

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:32.880
Oh, hello, Fred.

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:33.760
Won't you come in?

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:35.140
No, no, can't stay a minute.

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:38.200
Well, what's up?

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:41.020
Well, I hung around the sweatshop today after you left.

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:42.160
Weyer from New York.

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:43.440
Thought you'd like to know the scoop.

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:45.150
Well, yeah, I'd be delighted.

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:46.350
What is the scoop?

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:48.910
Well, it seems the big boys want the Farmington

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:51.890
reporter we've been working on on their desk first thing

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:52.570
Monday morning.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:53.240
Monday?

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:55.860
Well, that means we'd have to work over the weekend.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:59.190
I was going to take the boys up to Friends Lake.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:10:59.960
Of course, we're going to take the boys.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.150
You can afford to buck the big brass.

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:03.230
I'm just a little frog.

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:06.940
I'll do what I'll do, Ward.

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:09.220
I'll work on the report myself tomorrow.

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:11.490
I'll even sign your name if you want me to.

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:12.670
Thank you very much, Fred.

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:14.610
But I think I'd better meet you down at the office

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:15.690
first thing in the morning.

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:18.770
Must have to take the boys another time.

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:20.500
Well, too bad, Ward.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.100
But as Sir Walter Scott says,

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:26.980
the best laid plans of mice and men, Afgang Aglai.

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:28.180
That was Robert Burns.

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:31.450
Well, see you in the morning.

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:36.070
Are you sure that wasn't Sir Walter Scott?

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:38.200
Robert Burns.

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:41.240
If you're sure.

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:44.440
I'll have to ask Geraldine about that when I get home.

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:53.190
I guess you heard.

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:54.240
Yes.

00:11:55.530 --> 00:11:58.050
Oh, Lord, those boys are going to be so disappointed.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:11:59.980
They've told all of you.

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:07.350
friends all week about this camping trip no no dear well i i go up and tell them well they're asleep

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:18.250
now well i'll be up long before they are in the morning i'll tell them then would do you really

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:24.730
have to work tomorrow no no no i could just let it go give up my job sell the house and we could

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:29.490
all live in a tent at least that way the boys get to go camping

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:36.050
That was a very sweet thing to say.

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:47.200
I'm sorry, fellas.

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:48.680
I hope you understand.

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:49.920
I just can't help it.

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:52.520
Your father will take you camping next week.

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:54.490
That's okay, Dad.

00:12:55.310 --> 00:12:56.130
We understand.

00:12:58.250 --> 00:12:58.830
Sure we do.

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:05.630
Well, have a good day, fellas, and I'll see you later.

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:12.650
You know, boys, your father wanted

00:13:12.650 --> 00:13:14.630
to go on his camping trip just as much as you do.

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:18.120
But, well, when you're grown up, you just can't always

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:19.460
do everything you want to do.

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:22.410
Well, gee, Mom, that's the only reason I want to grow up.

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:24.520
So I can do everything I want to.

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:27.840
I don't think anyone ever grows up that much.

00:13:28.820 --> 00:13:29.900
Now, look, fellas, you get dressed.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:31.400
and come on down for breakfast, all right?

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:32.560
OK, Mom.

00:13:40.630 --> 00:13:41.110
Yeah, Beef?

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:43.260
I don't really understand.

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:46.600
Well, that's because you're just a kid.

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:48.170
Yeah, I guess so.

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:53.550
Well, at least the worms we caught.

00:13:54.260 --> 00:13:55.480
I think they'll ask them next week.

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:57.180
I don't think so.

00:13:57.570 --> 00:13:59.210
We better turn them loose and then catch them again

00:13:59.210 --> 00:13:59.830
if we need them.

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:03.600
Yeah, I guess Worm should have as much chance to have fun as anybody.

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:04.980
Yeah.

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:07.460
After the morning, you want to go to the movies?

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:10.580
Gee, Beaver, we told Eddie and all the guys we were going camping.

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:14.430
What would we say if they caught us there watching Vampires of the Amazon?

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:17.940
If we can't go to the movies, what are we going to do?

00:14:18.610 --> 00:14:20.190
Well, I guess we'll just have to do nothing.

00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:23.480
We'd rather do nothing when we'd have to go to school.

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:40.260
You've been keeping your supper hot. It's quarter seven. I thought you'd be home by five.

00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:42.590
Dear, Fred was helping me.

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:43.590
Oh.

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:45.670
Where are the boys?

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:47.880
The boys have gone camping.

00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:49.600
Camping? Where?

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:54.590
Well, they wanted to go up in the sand hills, but they are now roughing it in the wilds of our backyard.

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:56.560
No fooling.

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:59.980
Uh-huh. They picked the tent out in back of the garage and they took out their sleeping bags.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.020
and they're going to spend the night.

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:01.720
What are they doing?

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:03.860
Well, they came in and got some hamburger meat

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:05.120
so they could cook their own supper.

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:07.940
But they asked me if I'd mind not coming out.

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:08.610
Oh?

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.360
Well, he said it wouldn't seem like they were out in the wilds

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:13.540
if their mother stuck her head in every few minutes.

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:17.380
I think before I eat my supper, I'll just kind of wander out

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:18.260
and see how we're getting along.

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:20.430
Better wear your red honey hat.

00:15:20.930 --> 00:15:22.470
I think the beaver has a water pistol.

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:24.010
Well, I'll risk it.

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:34.410
Hey, who's out there?

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:36.050
Nobody here but us bears.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.810
Hi, Dad.

00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:40.510
Hi.

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:41.300
Hi, Dad.

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:43.620
I think it was Eddie or some of the kids messing around.

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:46.000
I'll just let you have it.

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:46.870
Oh.

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:51.860
Well, you, uh, you fellas did a pretty good job of getting this up.

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:55.600
Well, it fell down a couple of times,

00:15:55.660 --> 00:15:58.540
but then people stood in here and held it up while I drove in the stakes.

00:15:59.120 --> 00:15:59.400
Oh.

00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:02.210
Yeah, I'm just about the same size as your tent poles.

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:05.230
Well, you know, fellows, I'm still

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:06.490
sorry about that camping trip.

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:08.470
But we're going to make it for sure next weekend.

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:09.910
That's OK, Dad.

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:11.850
We're having a lot of fun out here.

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:13.000
Well, that's swell.

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:16.080
Fellows, I think I'd button up this tent flap, though.

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:17.880
It's beginning to blow up a little bit outside.

00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:18.600
OK, Dad.

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:20.450
If you smell anything brewed in the morning,

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:22.360
that's just us cooking our breakfast.

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:24.220
Oh, that fever.

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:25.730
Well, good night, fellas.

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:27.330
And if you need anything, just yell.

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:29.980
Gee, Dad, we can take care of our ..

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:32.160
We're just about grown up.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:34.480
Sure, Dad.

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:37.040
We learned how to do things for ourselves,

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:38.200
just like you said.

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:39.280
Oh, yeah.

00:16:40.390 --> 00:16:42.100
Yeah, I guess you are, Beaver.

00:16:43.030 --> 00:16:43.650
Good-bye, fellas.

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:44.190
Bye.

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:58.910
Hey, Beaver, where'd you put the eggs?

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:00.880
They're my knapsack.

00:17:01.590 --> 00:17:02.090
Oh, good.

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:07.270
In the morning, pretend these are eagle's eggs.

00:17:07.940 --> 00:17:10.380
We had to climb to the top of the mountain to get them out of the eagle's nest.

00:17:11.060 --> 00:17:12.440
What would you want to do that for?

00:17:12.830 --> 00:17:14.110
I got a scratch on my arm.

00:17:15.560 --> 00:17:17.120
We can pretend that's where the eagle clawed me.

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:20.260
Well, you can pretend what you want to, but I'm just going to eat them.

00:17:22.820 --> 00:17:25.700
And anyway, who ever heard of an eagle laying a hard-boiled egg?

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:31.580
It's a little dried out, but it's hot.

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:33.700
You know, the boys are doing great out there.

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:37.040
Paper said it may rain.

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:39.140
Think they'll be all right out there?

00:17:39.380 --> 00:17:41.000
Oh, they'll be perfectly all right.

00:17:41.420 --> 00:17:44.060
The thing for us to do is to leave them alone, not spoil their fun.

00:17:44.710 --> 00:17:45.610
I suppose so.

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:52.740
You know, you talk about Wally and the beaver.

00:17:53.260 --> 00:17:55.840
When I was a kid, Chuck Tollefson and I slept in a tree.

00:17:57.250 --> 00:17:58.150
You had a tree house?

00:17:59.240 --> 00:17:59.980
No, we just slept in a tree house.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:00.860
I slept in a tree one night.

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:03.520
What'd you do that for?

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:06.590
Well, we'd tell the kids at school that we'd slept in a tree.

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:09.520
We were quite the celebrities of the fourth grade.

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:13.290
A few days later, Lindbergh flew to the Atlantic

00:18:13.290 --> 00:18:14.530
and took the play away from us.

00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:18.210
You're making that up.

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:20.760
Oh, he really flew to the Atlantic.

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:21.840
It was in all the papers.

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:23.760
Want me some coffee?

00:18:43.670 --> 00:18:50.340
Yeah, you know, Eddie Haskell says if you touch the inside of a tent while it's raining, it'll start a leak.

00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:59.930
Add that Eddie. He thinks he knows everything. Will it really start a leak if you just touch it?

00:19:00.510 --> 00:19:01.290
I don't know.

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:02.450
That's what Eddie says.

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:12.810
Eddie was right.

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:16.170
There must have been a hole there or something.

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:19.400
He couldn't start a leak just by touching a tent like this.

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:25.480
Boy, that Eddie sure is a wise guy.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:30.920
Holly, look over there.

00:19:33.370 --> 00:19:34.130
So what?

00:19:34.290 --> 00:19:35.190
We're camping out.

00:19:35.390 --> 00:19:36.810
Little water's not going to hurt us.

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:39.300
Sure, it's not going to hurt us.

00:19:41.690 --> 00:19:42.850
We're going to get to sleep.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:49.100
It's not better with the light out.

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:51.560
We can't see the rain coming in.

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:05.240
Look at the way it's coming down.

00:20:07.020 --> 00:20:08.800
Lord, I think you ought to go out and tell those boys

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:09.540
to come in.

00:20:09.860 --> 00:20:11.940
June, I disappointed them about the camping trip.

00:20:12.040 --> 00:20:13.500
I can't go out there now and spoil

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:14.580
what little fun they are having.

00:20:15.390 --> 00:20:17.010
Anyway, there's nothing more exciting to a boy

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:18.910
than lying in a tent listening to the rain.

00:20:19.220 --> 00:20:21.800
But the wind, what if the tent blows over?

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:23.640
Just be lying there in the rain.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:25.640
Dear, they're right in their own backyard.

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:29.400
I suppose so.

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:37.560
I have to admit, I kind of wish they'd come in myself.

00:20:39.260 --> 00:20:40.260
We couldn't make them.

00:20:42.410 --> 00:20:43.090
They'll be all right.

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:45.580
I'm going to get a glass of hot milk and go up to bed.

00:20:59.390 --> 00:20:59.970
Sleep well.

00:21:00.090 --> 00:21:02.270
No, I'm not asleep.

00:21:02.410 --> 00:21:04.540
I thought you were asleep.

00:21:05.360 --> 00:21:07.540
No, I'm not asleep either.

00:21:09.470 --> 00:21:09.910
You OK?

00:21:11.190 --> 00:21:11.930
Oh, sure.

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:16.040
But it kind of feels like the water's

00:21:16.040 --> 00:21:17.520
coming to the sleeping bag with me.

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:20.610
I'd better turn up the lantern and see how we're doing.

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:24.220
I'm glad we stopped those leaks.

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:29.360
Well, this is something.

00:21:30.410 --> 00:21:30.930
Yeah.

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:35.360
I bet we got the only tent which went in water.

00:21:38.690 --> 00:21:40.010
Something bothering you, Wally?

00:21:40.940 --> 00:21:41.640
Well, heck no.

00:21:41.950 --> 00:21:43.810
You wouldn't be thinking about going in the house

00:21:43.810 --> 00:21:44.970
or anything like that, would you?

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:46.820
Oh, of course not.

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:49.940
We're camping out in any way.

00:21:50.260 --> 00:21:52.780
Even if we were thinking of going in, we couldn't go in.

00:21:53.500 --> 00:21:54.160
Well, sure.

00:21:56.850 --> 00:21:57.330
Couldn't we?

00:21:58.000 --> 00:21:59.280
Because we told Mom and Dad we could

00:21:59.280 --> 00:21:59.980
take care of our sick.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:04.180
If they ever saw us coming in on account of a little bit of rain,

00:22:04.670 --> 00:22:06.190
they'd think we were a couple of sissies.

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:08.570
Yeah, I guess you're right.

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:12.400
I think we better do something about this water.

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:15.980
Whatever we do, we better do it real quick.

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:29.970
Hey, dear, how are the boys doing this?

00:22:30.750 --> 00:22:32.350
They're fixing their breakfast out there.

00:22:36.090 --> 00:22:37.210
Glad they're all right.

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:39.920
That was an awful rain we had last night.

00:22:42.170 --> 00:22:43.430
We must have a leak.

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:47.120
There are wet spots all over the carpet on the stairs.

00:22:47.300 --> 00:22:47.580
Are there?

00:22:49.490 --> 00:22:50.950
I'm going to have to call Dunlap.

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:52.380
Have them check our roof.

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:54.400
Couldn't do that if I were you, dear.

00:22:57.220 --> 00:22:59.980
I think the boys made those spots on the carpet last night.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:01.840
night when they carry their sleeping bags upstairs.

00:23:02.180 --> 00:23:06.570
What happened?

00:23:08.300 --> 00:23:10.320
Must have looked out of that window two or three times

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:13.180
last night to see if that tent had blown over.

00:23:13.540 --> 00:23:14.400
It was about midnight.

00:23:14.950 --> 00:23:15.850
You were sound asleep.

00:23:18.130 --> 00:23:18.930
How'd they get in?

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:24.570
Well, I may have accidentally left the kitchen door unlocked.

00:23:26.210 --> 00:23:26.650
Accidentally?

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:31.680
I went back out when the rain stopped this morning.

00:23:31.820 --> 00:23:32.840
It was around 5 o'clock.

00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:38.680
Ward, how are you so sure those boys would come in?

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:41.040
Well, I wasn't, but I wanted the back door

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:42.280
unlocked in case they did.

00:23:44.520 --> 00:23:46.300
Sounds like you've been through this before.

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:50.960
Yeah, the time Chuck Tollison and I slept in that tree,

00:23:51.550 --> 00:23:52.970
after a couple of hours, his father

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:54.090
came out and made us come down.

00:23:56.060 --> 00:23:58.260
I'd give it anything if you just left the back door unlocked.

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:11.280
Yo, B, we leave for Sunday school.

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:13.850
You mean you guys took the sleeping bags

00:24:13.850 --> 00:24:15.450
and slept out in a tent last night?

00:24:16.120 --> 00:24:17.940
Yeah, we slept out there most of the night.

00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:19.540
It was real neat.

00:24:20.500 --> 00:24:22.620
Boy, my pop wouldn't let me sleep out in the rain.

00:24:22.960 --> 00:24:24.860
He'd have come out and dragged me out of the house

00:24:24.860 --> 00:24:27.060
and made me take a hot bath.

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:29.970
Didn't you?

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:31.320
Should Pop make you come in at all?

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:32.260
Heck no.

00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:36.840
Boy, I wish I had a father who never cared what I did.

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:40.400
Come on, we'll be late for Sunday school.

00:24:49.730 --> 00:24:51.310
He does kill what we do, doesn't he?

00:24:51.950 --> 00:24:52.850
He does, babe.

00:24:54.580 --> 00:24:56.560
Who do you think left the door unlocked last night?

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:30.440
You
